  "Notify users when tasks they care about change" is not one subsystem, it's roughly four: [1mtasks[0m (a model, storage, an
  editing UI), [1midentity[0m (who a user is, so "they" means something), [1mevents/subscriptions[0m (what counts as a change, who
  cares about which task), and [1mdelivery[0m (in-app, email, push). Trying to spec all of that at once produces a spec too
  vague to build from. So I'd decompose into sub-projects, spec the first one properly, and let notifications land once
  there's something to notify about.

  Before I can propose that decomposition, one question determines its entire shape.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether users share tasks with each other. That decides whether you need a server at all.

  - [1mMulti-user / collaborative[0m — tasks are assigned, shared, commented on. "Tasks they care about" means [3mother people's[0m
    changes reaching you. Requires a backend, accounts, auth, a database, and a real event pipeline. Expensive, and
    essentially impossible to retrofit onto a browser-only app later — this is the one choice here that is genuinely
    hard to reverse.
  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — one person's task list in localStorage. "Notifications" means reminders: due dates,
    overdue items, via the browser Notification API. No server, no accounts. Buildable in days, and honest about what
    this repo is today.
  - [1mSingle-user now, server later[0m — build local-first, but keep the data model and event shape server-ready. Buys
    optionality at the cost of some abstraction you may never use.

  My recommendation is [1msingle-user, browser-only[0m, unless you actually have collaborators in mind. The word "users" in
  your request could mean either, and the difference is roughly a week of work versus a quarter. If it's genuinely
  collaborative, say so and I'll plan for the backend — I just don't want to infer a multi-tenant system from an empty
  HTML file.
